course: dictionary
lesson: 3484
title: Famine: a time without food
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A famine is a time when a large number of people in one place do not have enough food, often for months. Many may become ill or die. Famine is a big word for a whole period of hunger, not just one hungry afternoon.
explain: Read the words around it. «From 1845 a potato disease destroyed Ireland's main food; about a million people died in the Great Famine.» The main food was destroyed and people died, so a famine must be a time when there is far too little to eat.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. «Starving» after school lunch is hyperbole, a joke-sized exaggeration. But in a famine people really had no food for weeks, so the word is literal. Always ask whether the text means it exactly or as an exaggeration.
fact: A famine is a time when many people do not have enough food, often for months.
fact: Famines often follow drought, war or crop failure.
fact: «Famine relief» means help, such as food and money, sent to people suffering in a famine.
fact: The Great Famine in Ireland began in 1845, when a potato disease destroyed the main food.
fact: Famine is often used without a or s, as in «suffer from famine», but «a famine» names one particular time.
exercise: «The Great Famine in Ireland began when the potato crop failed.» What is a famine? | A large party with lots of food | *A time when many people do not have enough food | A sudden storm at sea | A type of farm machine
exercise: Charities send food to people in a famine. Which phrase means that kind of help? | famine school | famine party | *famine relief | famine tea
exercise: «In a history book, people had no food for weeks.» Is this describing a famine literally or as hyperbole? | Figurative, a joke | *Literally, it really happened | Hyperbole, just an exaggeration | Pretend, like a story
test: Famines often follow which events? | Birthday parties | Sports days | *Drought, war or crop failure | Holidays at the seaside
test: What did the Great Famine in Ireland begin with? | A new king | *A potato disease that destroyed the main food | Heavy snow | A school trip
exam: «The town was hit by a famine that lasted for months.» Which meaning fits best? | *A time when many people lack food, often for months | A kind of cloth | A big party for the whole town | A fast train
